 ## Subtopics
 
-### GNeRF
-GNeRF is a two-phase framework that jointly optimizes camera poses and neural radiance fields from uncalibrated images using adversarial training [7359-gnerf.md]. It is designed for scenarios where Structure-from-Motion (SfM) fails due to low texture or complex outside-in configurations [7359-gnerf.md]. Unlike methods requiring rough initialization, GNeRF uses an inversion network to decouple pose estimation from direct photometric gradient propagation, which is prone to local minima [7359-gnerf.md]. It builds on [Radiance-field optimization](44-radiance-field-optimization.md) by introducing a GAN-based generator-discriminator pair [7359-gnerf.md].
-
-### GANeRF
-GANeRF optimizes a 3D radiance field using a 2D adversarial loss to enforce 3D-consistent photorealism [7571-ganerf.md]. It differs from non-adversarial refinement methods like NeRFLiX by using direct backpropagation from a discriminator to a full-resolution NeRF [7571-ganerf.md]. It builds on [Neural Radiance Fields](4972-neural-radiance-fields.md) by integrating a discriminator into the training loop with adversarial, VGG perceptual, and gradient penalty losses [7571-ganerf.md].
-
-### Semantic Pseudo Label with Local Texture Guidance
-This variant uses a patch discriminator to regularize 2D image fidelity, addressing view-inconsistent rendered color and texture [S1]. To overcome discriminator memorization due to limited training data, it employs differentiable augmentation $T$ applied to both real and fake samples [S1]. The GAN loss components are:
-$$
-\mathcal{L}_\text{D} =\mathbb{E}_{\boldsymbol{x} \sim p_{\text{data}}(\boldsymbol{x})}\left[f_{D}(-D(T(\boldsymbol{x})))\right] +\mathbb{E}_{\boldsymbol{z} \sim p(\boldsymbol{z})}\left[f_{D}(D(T(G(\boldsymbol{z}))))\right]
-$$
-$$
-\mathcal{L}_\text{G} =\mathbb{E}_{\boldsymbol{z} \sim p(\boldsymbol{z})}\left[f_{G}(-D(T(G(\boldsymbol{z}))))\right]
-$$
-$$
-\mathcal{L}_\text{adv} = \mathcal{L}_\text{D} + \mathcal{L}_\text{G}
-$$
-where $T$ refers to the augmentation applied on both real and fake samples [S1]. The GAN framework is trained using Hinge loss, so $f_D(x) = \max(0, 1 + x)$ and $f_G(x) = x$ [S1]. The discriminator architecture consists of a cascade of convolutional layers [S1].
-
-### No GAN Inversion
-This variant removes the GAN inversion step from the training pipeline [S2]. Renderings become blurry and uncanny because this step connects π-GAN training and reconstruction, significantly affecting overall performance [S2].
-
-## Background
-The integration of Generative Adversarial Networks (GANs) into volume rendering and NeRF optimization emerged to address the "blurry" or artifact-prone results of standard photometric loss minimization [S2]. Early works like SinNeRF [S1] demonstrated that adversarial training with local texture guidance could recover geometry and poses from uncalibrated single images. Subsequent works like Pix2NeRF [S2] focused on unsupervised conditional π-GAN translation for single-image to NeRF translation, while GANeRF [7571-ganerf.md] refined existing NeRFs for higher photorealism. The approach has become a standard component in high-fidelity radiance field training pipelines, often combined with perceptual losses and gradient penalties to stabilize the adversarial game [G].
